turnkey-lapp-19.0 (1) turnkey; urgency=low

  * LAPP is rebuilt as a child of the apache-php layer with PostgreSQL
    carried as a fab unit, instead of a single recipe on core that installs
    the web half itself. Decision 0013 is the reason: a database is a
    component, so LAMP and LAPP share one web layer rather than each being
    the child of its own database. The web half is no longer built twice.

        git clone --branch v1.0.0 \
            https://github.com/keel-linux/unit-postgresql.git unit.d/postgresql
        bt-layer lapp --parent apache-php

  * Two artefacts come out of this one recipe, split by content and not by
    topology, and the names say what is in them:

        lapp          Apache, PHP and a local PostgreSQL server
        lapp-client   the same stack with the client and the drivers and no
                      local server, for the database that lives elsewhere

    They differ by exactly one thing in the product directory: whether
    unit.d/postgresql is there. Nothing in the recipe asks which artefact is
    being built by name; conf.d/main reads what dpkg says the image
    contains, so an artefact that shipped a server it was not meant to have
    would fail its own build.

  * This recipe is keel-lamp's with the database named differently. The
    Makefile and the plan are the same file once the four database packages
    are named by their role, and conf.d/main is the same script with two
    blocks that differ, both because the two components are not the same:

      - the listen addresses. unit-postgresql writes them itself, so this
        recipe only checks the line is there; unit-mariadb does not, so
        keel-lamp has to write the equivalent file.
      - the build time credential. unit-postgresql's conf script gives the
        superuser the password 'postgres' when the build names none, so
        this recipe sets it to NULL afterwards; keel-lamp has no equivalent
        step, because the shared tree's conf/adminer-mysql creates its
        account with a value from mcookie that nobody records.

    Both are asymmetries in the components, not in the composition, and
    both are in README.rst with what would remove them.

  * The appliance's own landing page, /var/www/index.php, carries the mark
    from docs/brand of the handbook. It is the appliance's page and not the
    operator's site, which is the one place the brand manual asks for the
    mark to stay until it is replaced, and the page says which file to
    replace. The host name it prints comes from the request and is escaped
    before it reaches the document, which the upstream page did not do.

  * The database password is the component's path: firstboot.d/35pgsqlpass,
    which unit-postgresql ships, reads DB_PASS from the declarative first
    boot and gives it to the postgres role. The layer is published with that
    role unable to authenticate.

  * The cluster answers on ::1 and on 127.0.0.1, as literal addresses, which
    the component sets and this recipe checks. Debian's default,
    listen_addresses = 'localhost', binds the IPv4 loopback only, because
    /etc/hosts maps ::1 to ip6-localhost and never to localhost.

  * Four packages the two upstream recipes ship are deliberately not here,
    each in plan/main with its reason: python3-pygresql (this stack's
    second Python driver, superseded by psycopg2), and LAMP's
    libapache2-mod-python, php-xdebug and php-pear. Upstream LAPP's
    removelist is gone too: it removed /var/www/index.html, which no layer
    in this chain creates. Keeping any of them would make one of the two
    recipes differ from the other in something that is not the database.

 -- Marcos Méndez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Sun, 27 Sep 2026 23:00:00 +0000
